<div><html><body><h1>Slack Service Refactor Plan</h1><p><strong>Target:</strong> <code>src/slack/slack.service.ts</code></p><p><strong>Goal:</strong> reduce the size and responsibility concentration in <code>SlackService</code> without changing user-facing behavior, Slack workflows, or scheduled job outcomes.</p><h2>1. Problem statement</h2><p>The current <code>SlackService</code> mixes multiple concerns in one class:</p><ul><li>Slack Bolt app bootstrapping and event registration</li><li>help and entry-point interactions</li><li>PR submission and edit flows</li><li>review status updates and authorization feedback</li><li>notification composition and dispatch</li><li>reminder orchestration</li><li>weekly report generation</li><li>Slack message formatting decisions</li></ul><p>This makes the file hard to test, risky to change, and expensive to extend.</p><h2>2. Refactor objectives</h2><ul><li>Preserve all current Slack behavior and message flows</li><li>Separate orchestration from formatting and domain decisions</li><li>Make handler logic individually testable</li><li>Reduce the main service to a thin composition layer</li><li>Clarify dependencies between Slack, pull request domain logic, and scheduled jobs</li></ul><h2>3. Non-goals</h2><ul><li>No product behavior changes in the first refactor pass</li><li>No migration away from NestJS, Bolt, or Mongoose</li><li>No redesign of pull request lifecycle rules in <code>PullRequestsService</code></li><li>No large UX rewrite of Slack blocks in this phase</li></ul><h2>4. Current responsibility map</h2><ul><li><strong>Transport/bootstrap:</strong> receiver/app creation, command/action/view registration, app exposure</li><li><strong>Interaction handlers:</strong> app mention, DM message, modal trigger, submission, edit, update, review update, comment status update</li><li><strong>Message updates:</strong> channel message updates, DM success message updates, ephemeral authorization errors</li><li><strong>Notifications:</strong> review outcome notifications, reminder notifications</li><li><strong>Scheduled flows:</strong> review reminders, weekly report</li><li><strong>Formatting:</strong> notification strings, weekly report block structure, block selection</li></ul><h2>5. Proposed target structure</h2><p>Create smaller focused units under <code>src/slack</code>:</p><ul><li><code>src/slack/slack.service.ts</code> — thin facade/composition root</li><li><code>src/slack/slack.app.factory.ts</code> — Bolt receiver/app creation and registration wiring</li><li><code>src/slack/slack.config.ts</code> — typed Slack config access</li><li><code>src/slack/handlers/slack-help.handler.ts</code> — app mention and DM help flows</li><li><code>src/slack/handlers/slack-submission.handler.ts</code> — submit/edit/update modal flows</li><li><code>src/slack/handlers/slack-review.handler.ts</code> — review status and comment resolution flows</li><li><code>src/slack/services/slack-message.service.ts</code> — Slack chat update/post wrappers</li><li><code>src/slack/services/slack-notification.service.ts</code> — notification and reminder composition/dispatch</li><li><code>src/slack/services/slack-weekly-report.service.ts</code> — weekly report query, aggregation, and posting</li><li><code>src/slack/services/slack-user.service.ts</code> — Slack user lookup and normalization wrapper around <code>getUserInfo</code></li><li><code>src/slack/presenters/slack-notification.presenter.ts</code> — pure builders for notification text/block payloads</li><li><code>src/slack/presenters/slack-weekly-report.presenter.ts</code> — pure builders for report blocks</li><li><code>src/slack/types/*.ts</code> — handler payload and internal DTO types</li></ul><h2>6. Recommended responsibility split</h2><h3>6.1 SlackService after refactor</h3><ul><li>Own the public entry points used by the controller or app bootstrap</li><li>Expose <code>getApp()</code>, <code>triggerReviewReminders()</code>, and <code>triggerWeeklyReport()</code> by delegating</li><li>Avoid direct business logic or message construction</li></ul><h3>6.2 Handlers</h3><ul><li><strong>Help handler:</strong> mention and DM response logic</li><li><strong>Submission handler:</strong> open modal, submit PR, edit PR, update PR</li><li><strong>Review handler:</strong> update review status, update comment status, submission message update decisions</li></ul><p>Each handler should focus on one interaction family and delegate persistence and messaging.</p><h3>6.3 Services</h3><ul><li><strong>SlackMessageService:</strong> wrapper around <code>client.chat.postMessage</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>postEphemeral</code>, <code>getPermalink</code></li><li><strong>SlackNotificationService:</strong> dispatch review outcome notifications and reminders</li><li><strong>SlackWeeklyReportService:</strong> weekly report orchestration and Slack posting</li><li><strong>SlackUserService:</strong> fetch merger/reviewer/author user records and map reviewer state</li></ul><h3>6.4 Presenters</h3><ul><li>Keep text and block-generation logic pure and stateless</li><li>Return payload objects only</li><li>Contain string templates now embedded in <code>handleNotificationDispatch</code>, <code>handleReminderDispatch</code>, and <code>buildWeeklyReportGroupBlocks</code></li></ul><h2>7. Refactor approach</h2><p>Use an incremental strangler approach instead of a full rewrite.</p><h3>Phase 1: Stabilize and prepare</h3><ul><li>Add characterization tests around current flows before moving code</li><li>Capture expected behavior for:</li><li>submit PR</li><li>update PR</li><li>review status update authorization failures</li><li>notification dispatch cases</li><li>reminder dispatch cases</li><li>weekly report grouping and totals</li></ul><p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> safety net tests for current behavior.</p><h3>Phase 2: Extract pure formatting logic</h3><ul><li>Move notification text/block building into presenter functions</li><li>Move weekly report block building into presenter functions</li><li>Keep public behavior unchanged</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> lower cognitive load, easy unit testing.</p><h3>Phase 3: Extract Slack client wrapper</h3><ul><li>Introduce <code>SlackMessageService</code> for post/update/ephemeral/permalink operations</li><li>Replace direct <code>client.chat.*</code> calls in handlers with wrapper methods</li><li>Normalize channel selection and common error handling</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> less duplicated Slack API usage and easier mocking.</p><h3>Phase 4: Extract submission flow</h3><ul><li>Move <code>handleSubmitModalTrigger</code>, <code>handleSubmitPullRequest</code>, <code>handleEditSubmission</code>, and <code>handleUpdatePullRequest</code> into <code>SlackSubmissionHandler</code></li><li>Extract user enrichment logic into <code>SlackUserService</code></li><li>Keep <code>PullRequestsService</code> as domain owner for validation and persistence</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> one place for PR submission/edit workflow.</p><h3>Phase 5: Extract review and notification flow</h3><ul><li>Move <code>handleReviewStatusUpdate</code>, <code>handleSubmissionMessageUpdate</code>, and <code>handleUpdateCommentStatus</code> into <code>SlackReviewHandler</code></li><li>Move notification dispatch into <code>SlackNotificationService</code></li><li>Centralize authorization error responses</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> clearer ownership for state transition side effects.</p><h3>Phase 6: Extract scheduled jobs</h3><ul><li>Move reminder orchestration to <code>SlackNotificationService</code> or dedicated <code>SlackReminderService</code></li><li>Move weekly report logic to <code>SlackWeeklyReportService</code></li><li>Keep controller unchanged initially</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> cron-triggered paths isolated from interaction handlers.</p><h3>Phase 7: Simplify module wiring</h3><ul><li>Register extracted providers in <code>slack.module.ts</code></li><li>Consider importing <code>PullRequestsModule</code> instead of directly providing <code>PullRequestsService</code> if supported by the existing module exports</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> cleaner dependency boundaries.</p><h2>8. Suggested file plan for MVP refactor</h2><p>If we want the smallest useful first cut, implement only these extractions first:</p><ul><li><code>slack-message.service.ts</code></li><li><code>slack-notification.service.ts</code></li><li><code>slack-weekly-report.service.ts</code></li><li><code>slack-review.handler.ts</code></li></ul><p>This gives the biggest maintainability gain with moderate delivery risk.</p><h2>9. Example target dependency flow</h2><ul><li><code>SlackController</code> → <code>SlackService</code></li><li><code>SlackService</code> → registers Bolt handlers and delegates to extracted handlers/services</li><li><code>SlackSubmissionHandler</code> → <code>PullRequestsService</code>, <code>SlackUserService</code>, <code>SlackMessageService</code></li><li><code>SlackReviewHandler</code> → <code>PullRequestsService</code>, <code>SlackMessageService</code>, <code>SlackNotificationService</code></li><li><code>SlackWeeklyReportService</code> → <code>PullRequestsService</code>, presenter</li><li><code>SlackNotificationService</code> → presenter, <code>SlackMessageService</code></li></ul><h2>10. Key risks and mitigations</h2><ul><li><strong>Risk:</strong> breaking Slack action/view bindings during extraction.<br/> <strong>Mitigation:</strong> keep event names unchanged and move one handler family at a time.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> behavior drift in notification text or block payloads.<br/> <strong>Mitigation:</strong> characterization tests for presenter outputs and smoke-test in Slack sandbox.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> hidden coupling through shared mutable objects like <code>structuredValues</code>.<br/> <strong>Mitigation:</strong> introduce typed DTOs and avoid in-place mutation where possible.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> dependency duplication in Nest module wiring.<br/> <strong>Mitigation:</strong> extract providers in small steps and verify module exports/imports.</li></ul><h2>11. Acceptance criteria for the refactor</h2><ul><li><code>slack.service.ts</code> is reduced to orchestration-focused code and is materially smaller</li><li>Notification text/block generation is outside the main service</li><li>Weekly report logic is outside the main service</li><li>Review flow logic is outside the main service</li><li>Existing cron schedules and Slack event names remain unchanged</li><li>Submit, edit, review, reminder, and weekly report flows still work end to end</li><li>New units have direct automated tests</li></ul><h2>12. Proposed backlog</h2><ol><li><strong>P0:</strong> add characterization tests for current Slack flows</li><li><strong>P0:</strong> extract presenters for notifications and weekly report blocks</li><li><strong>P1:</strong> add <code>SlackMessageService</code> wrapper</li><li><strong>P1:</strong> extract review flow into <code>SlackReviewHandler</code></li><li><strong>P1:</strong> extract weekly report into <code>SlackWeeklyReportService</code></li><li><strong>P2:</strong> extract submission/edit flow into <code>SlackSubmissionHandler</code></li><li><strong>P2:</strong> introduce typed config and DTOs</li><li><strong>P3:</strong> revisit module boundaries with <code>PullRequestsModule</code> exports/imports</li></ol><h2>13. Recommendation</h2><p>Start with a low-risk refactor slice:</p><ol><li>tests</li><li>presenters</li><li>message wrapper</li><li>weekly report extraction</li><li>review flow extraction</li></ol><p>This sequence improves maintainability quickly while minimizing the chance of breaking the core PR submission flow.</p><h2>14. Next implementation step</h2><p>Create the first refactor PR with this scope only:</p><ul><li>add tests for current weekly report and notification outputs</li><li>extract <code>slack-notification.presenter.ts</code></li><li>extract <code>slack-weekly-report.presenter.ts</code></li><li>wire <code>SlackWeeklyReportService</code> into <code>SlackService</code></li></ul><p>This is the best first milestone because it removes isolated logic with relatively low runtime risk.</p></body></html></div>